      "answer": "No, the kinetic energy of the book has **not disappeared**.\n\nAccording to the **Law of Conservation of Energy**, energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed from one form to another. When the book stops, its kinetic energy (energy of motion) is converted into other forms of energy.\n\nHere is where the energy goes:\n\n1.  **Heat (Thermal Energy)**: The primary reason the book stops is friction. As the book slides across the rough table, the surfaces of the book and the table rub against each other. This friction converts the kinetic energy into **heat**, which warms up the book and the table.\n2.  **Sound Energy**: The rubbing motion also creates vibrations in the air and the table, producing a sound. This is **sound energy**.\n3.  **Deformation**: If the book is not perfectly rigid, some of the energy may also be used to slightly deform the surfaces of the book and the table.\n\nIn summary, the kinetic energy of the book is transformed into **heat** and **sound** energy.",
